  "checkpoint_step": 3,
  "checkpoint_text": "Step 1: For the congruence \\(8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}\\) to have a solution, \\(8\\) must have an inverse modulo \\(p\\). This is true if and only if \\(\\gcd(8, p) = 1\\).\n\nStep 2: Since \\(8 = 2^3\\), the only prime \\(p\\) for which \\(\\gcd(8, p) \\neq 1\\) is \\(p = 2\\). For any other prime \\(p\\), \\(\\gcd(8, p) = 1\\), meaning \\(8\\) has an inverse modulo \\(p\\), and thus the congruence has a solution.\n\nStep 3: Therefore, the only prime \\(p\\) for which the congruence \\(8x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p}\\) has no solutions is \\(p = 2\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{2}",
